\subsection{A narrowly technical confidentiality route}
Support, fair process and restorative justice are not determined by a graph theorem. The source supplies no single mathematical acceptance criterion for those community decisions. A limited technical facet is whether information sent through a support workflow can reach an unintended recipient. Define a finite directed graph whose vertices are communication locations and whose edges permit copying a particular sensitive message. A source vertex $s$ initially holds the message. Let $A$ be the locations authorized to receive it, with $s\in A$. Assume each permitted edge may eventually be used, messages can be copied without modification, and there are no other routes.
\begin{proposition}
No allowed sequence of transmissions leaks the message outside $A$ if and only if every vertex reachable from $s$ belongs to $A$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the number of transmissions: every location holding the message is either $s$ or was reached by an edge from an earlier holder, so it has a directed path from $s$. If all reachable vertices lie in $A$, no sequence leaks. Conversely, if a path $s=v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_k$ ends outside $A$, use its edges successively. Each transmission is permitted by the model, and after $k$ transmissions the endpoint holds the message outside $A$.
\end{proof}
Reachability is computable by starting with $\{s\}$ and repeatedly adding out-neighbors; on a finite graph this stops, includes only path endpoints by induction, and includes every path endpoint by induction on path length. With adjacency lists and each vertex processed once, its operation count is $O(|V|+|E|)$.
